<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">382–<a href="/years/301-bce">301 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Antigonus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antigonus I Monophthalmus, the 'One-Eyed', was a Macedonian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who became one of the most powerful successors after Alexander's death. He sought to reunite the empire and expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> for a time. His defeat and death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE helped secure the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b>, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed Phrygia since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 302 BCE, fearing <b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b>, who had put a kinsman to death, he fled east with a few followers to the stronghold of Kimiata in Paphlagonia and built up a following among the peoples of north-eastern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hellenistic kings Miletus was usually a nominally free city that passed between <b>Antigonus</b>, Lysimachus, the Ptolemies and the Seleucids; its citizens honoured Antiochus II with the title Theos, god, for removing the tyrant Timarchus around 259 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great took Thracian contingents to Asia, and after his death in 323 BCE Seuthes III fought an indecisive battle against Lysimachus, the new Macedonian governor of Thrace, and later sided with <b>Antigonus</b> against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the royal hypaspists under Alexander and the Companion cavalry under the regent Perdiccas, was appointed satrap of Babylonia in 320 BCE but was driven out by the dominant Diadochos <b>Antigonus I Monophthalmus</b> in 316 BCE, spending several years in exile at the court of Ptolemy I in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He was an ally of Lysimachus, Seleucus and Ptolemy against <b>Antigonus</b> I.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
